---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="llms.txt Syntax and Format Rules" description="llms.txt syntax reference: required structure, heading levels, link format, optional sections, and validation rules."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["llms.txt Syntax"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">llms.txt Syntax</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">llms.txt Syntax and Format Rules</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      llms.txt is deliberately simple: GitHub-flavored markdown with a fixed shape. The constraint
      is the point, machines parse it without ambiguity. Here is the whole grammar.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-required-shape">The required shape</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rules-that-matter">Rules that matter</a></li>
        <li><a href="#common-mistakes">Common mistakes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#validate-and-generate">Validate and generate</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-required-shape">The required shape</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft"># Site Name (required H1, first line)

> One-sentence summary of the site. (required blockquote, immediately after)

## Optional section
- [Link title](https://absolute-url): description after the colon
- [Another](https://example.com/page): another description

## Another optional section
- [More](https://example.com/more): grouped links by topic</pre>
      <h2 id="rules-that-matter">Rules that matter</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Exactly one H1, first line: the site name.</li>
        <li>One blockquote line directly beneath: the thesis of the site.</li>
        <li>H2 sections group related links; keep them thematic (Docs, Tools, Pricing).</li>
        <li>Links are markdown with <strong>absolute URLs</strong>; the text after the colon is the one-line description.</li>
        <li>Plain UTF-8 text at <code>/llms.txt</code>; no auth, no redirects, no HTML wrapper.</li>
        <li>Optional: a link to <code>llms-full.txt</code> for the complete flattened content.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Relative URLs; consumers may not have a base context.</li>
        <li>Marketing fluff in the blockquote; it should state what the site <em>is</em>.</li>
        <li>Linking everything instead of the important pages; curation is the value.</li>
        <li>Forgetting the file is publicly cached: update it with releases, not per-deploy.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="validate-and-generate">Validate and generate</h2>
      <p>
        Build a spec-correct file with the <a href="/llms-txt-generator/">llms.txt generator</a>, and
        confirm its presence (plus your robots.txt posture) with the
        <a href="/ai-readiness-checker/">AI readiness audit</a>.
      </p>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/what-is-llms-txt/">What is llms.txt?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/robots-txt-guide/">robots.txt: the complete guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
